Outcome-Centric Computing: A Formal Theory of AI-Native Software Systems

Dissertation — Version 4.0

AI : chatGpt, Deepseek, Gemini 

ouadi Maakoul 

Abstract

This dissertation develops a formal theory of computation for systems whose correctness cannot be characterized by prescribed input–output mappings alone. The central object is the outcome-semantic quotient space \mathcal T/\!\sim_O, which expresses the fundamental structural fact that many behaviorally distinct trajectories may collapse to the same acceptable semantic outcome. We define behavioral freedom relative to a declared reference measure on the trajectory space, prove the existence and monotonicity of the Freedom–Reliability Frontier under explicit compactness and semicontinuity assumptions, characterize the shadow price of reliability via nonsmooth envelope methods, and establish a three-regime Verification–Recovery Tradeoff Theorem with global uniqueness via strict concavity. Recovery is shown to expand the feasible policy set monotonically, though its effect on frontier curvature remains a conjecture. Dynamic authority is formulated as a Markov decision problem. The deterministic function-based specification model is recovered as a limiting special case. The theory is positioned as a unified formalism whose primitive correctness object is the acceptable trajectory set \Phi^{-1}(\Omega), rather than a singleton output.

---

Chapter 1: The Abstraction Shift

1.1 The Deterministic Function-Based Abstraction

The canonical deterministic function-based abstraction represents a software system as a function f:X\to Y. Correctness is defined by equality f(x)=y^\ast. This abstraction rests on three assumptions:

· A1 (Unique correct output). For every input x, there is a unique correct output y^\ast.
· A2 (Pre-specifiable behavior). The mapping f can be fully specified before execution.
· A3 (Execution correctness). Correctness is a property of the execution itself.

These assumptions are often insufficient for a class of AI-native systems. For tasks such as "organize this dataset," "write a defensible analysis," or "negotiate a solution," there is no unique correct output. The environment is uncertain. A system may fail initially yet recover. Correctness at the level of execution is not correctness at the level of the outcome.

1.2 The Outcome-Centric Alternative

An outcome-centric system is specified by an intent I, a context C, and constraints K. The intent induces an acceptable outcome set \Omega(I,C,K)\subseteq\mathcal O. The system searches a trajectory space \mathcal T for a trajectory \tau satisfying

\Phi(\tau)\in\Omega(I,C,K),

where \Phi:\mathcal T\to\mathcal O is the outcome map. The fundamental structural fact is that \Phi is generally non-injective: many behaviorally distinct trajectories may map to the same semantic outcome. This yields the central diagram

\mathcal T \overset{\Phi}{\longrightarrow} \mathcal O \overset{/\,\equiv_O}{\longrightarrow} \mathcal M_O,

where \mathcal M_O=\mathcal T/\!\sim_O is the outcome-semantic quotient space. The primitive correctness object is not a singleton output but the acceptable trajectory set

\boxed{\mathcal T_\Omega=\Phi^{-1}(\Omega)\subseteq\mathcal T.}

1.3 The Central Research Question

\boxed{\text{Under what conditions can behavioral freedom be increased without violating probabilistic guarantees on acceptable outcomes?}}

1.4 Layered Structure of the Theory

The theory is organized into four layers:

Semantic layer. (\Gamma,\Phi,\equiv_O) — what counts as success.

Behavioral layer. (\mathcal B_{\text{env}},\Pi,\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}) — what behaviors are possible.

Reliability layer. (\mathcal V,\rho) — how failures are detected and recovered.

Authority layer. \mathcal H — who controls decisions.

Outcome-centric computing is the integrated study of these four layers. AI-native software is the motivating application domain, not the mathematical restriction of the theory.

