Chapter 2: Related Formalisms and Precise Demarcation

2.1 Purpose

A hostile committee member will place MDPs, POMDPs, formal methods, runtime verification, fault-tolerant control, program synthesis, and agentic architectures on a single slide and ask: What is new here? This chapter answers with precise demarcation.

2.2 Existing Formalisms

Formalism Primary Object Correctness Criterion Relationship to This Work
Classical computability f:X\to Y f(x)=y^\ast Degenerate correctness case of \Omega=\{y^\ast\}
Nondeterministic computation R\subseteq X\times Y (x,y)\in R Singleton acceptable set, no stochastic search
MDP / POMDP (S,A,P,R) Optimal policy \pi^\ast Provides stochastic search substrate
Constrained MDP (S,A,P,R,c) Optimal policy subject to constraints Provides Pareto frontier structure
Outcome-equivalent MDP abstraction Abstract MDP M_\phi Outcome-equivalent state abstraction Provides state abstraction; not trajectory-level quotient
Runtime verification Monitor M Detection of violation Provides verification operator \mathcal V
Fault-tolerant control Redundant subsystems Fault recovery Provides recovery kernels
Program synthesis \varphi\to P \llbracket P\rrbracket\models\varphi Synthesis before execution

2.3 The Novelty Claim

The contribution is not the invention of any single component. Constrained MDPs already possess Pareto frontiers and shadow prices. Fault-tolerant MDPs already possess recovery kernels. Runtime verification already possesses monitors. Outcome-equivalent MDP abstractions already exist.

The contribution is a unified formalism whose primitive correctness object is \Phi^{-1}(\Omega), rather than a singleton output. The dissertation investigates whether this combination of objects yields a distinct analytical framework. The eventual claim is:

\boxed{\text{The contribution is a unified formalism whose primitive correctness object is }\Phi^{-1}(\Omega),\text{ rather than a singleton output.}}

This is a falsifiable claim. If every major result concerning outcome-centric computing can be expressed as a straightforward reformulation of existing constrained-MDP, stochastic-control, runtime-verification, or fault-tolerant-control results without requiring the outcome-semantic quotient \mathcal M_O, then the proposed framework does not constitute a distinct theoretical abstraction.

2.4 The Outcome Evaluator Problem

If \Phi(\tau) requires an intelligent evaluator, the framework may have moved the difficulty rather than solved it. We distinguish three regimes: cheap and objective, expensive but decidable, and contested or approximate. The theory's strongest claims apply to the first two regimes. The third requires a meta-level theory of evaluator reliability R_\Phi, treated as future work.

