Chapter 3: Formal Foundations

3.1 Trajectory Spaces and Measures

Assumption 3.1. One of the following holds:

· (D) Discrete. \mathcal X and \mathcal A are finite or countable; \mathcal T=\bigcup_{T=0}^{T_{\max}}(\mathcal X\times\mathcal A)^T\times\mathcal X with counting measure \mu.
· (C) Continuous. \mathcal X\subseteq\mathbb R^d and \mathcal A\subseteq\mathbb R^k are compact; T\le T_{\max}; \mathcal T carries a standard Borel structure with a reference measure \nu.

Under (D), entropy is Shannon entropy. Under (C), differential entropy is used only as an operational metric.

Definition 3.1 (Trajectory). A trajectory is a finite sequence \tau=(s_0,a_0,\dots,s_T).

Axiom A1 (Trajectory realizability). For every policy \Pi and every (I,C,E), P_\Pi(\cdot\mid I,C,E) is a well-defined probability measure on (\mathcal T,\Sigma_{\mathcal T}).

3.2 Support

Definition 3.2 (Support).

· Under (D): \operatorname{supp}(P_\Pi)=\{\tau\in\mathcal T:P_\Pi(\tau)>0\}.
· Under (C): \operatorname{supp}(P_\Pi) is the topological support, the smallest closed set of full P_\Pi-measure.

Remark 3.1. The naive definition \{\tau:P_\Pi(\tau)>0\} is empty under continuous measures for all non-degenerate policies. Definition 3.2 repairs this.

3.3 Outcome Semantics

Definition 3.3 (Outcome map). \Phi:\mathcal T\to\mathcal O is measurable with factorization \Phi=g\circ h.

Definition 3.4 (Acceptable outcome set). \Omega(I,C,K)\subseteq\mathcal O.

Definition 3.5 (Acceptable trajectory set).

\boxed{\mathcal T_\Omega=\Phi^{-1}(\Omega)\subseteq\mathcal T.}

Definition 3.6 (Outcome equivalence). \tau_i\sim_O\tau_j\iff\Phi(\tau_i)\equiv_O\Phi(\tau_j).

Definition 3.7 (Outcome-semantic quotient space).

\boxed{\mathcal M_O=\mathcal T/\!\sim_O.}

Remark 3.2. \mathcal M_O is the outcome-semantic quotient space. It provides a compressed representation of trajectories that are indistinguishable under the chosen outcome equivalence relation. It is not a smooth manifold in general; it carries the quotient Borel structure.

Remark 3.3. The hidden foundational object is the equivalence relation \equiv_O. The quotient \mathcal M_O is only as meaningful as \equiv_O.

3.4 Behavioral Space Hierarchy

Definition 3.8 (Possible behavioral space).

\boxed{\mathcal B_{\text{poss}}=\mathcal B_{\text{env}}.}

This is the set of trajectories physically realizable under environment dynamics, independent of policy.

Definition 3.9 (Reachable behavioral space).

\boxed{\mathcal B_{\text{reach}}(\Pi)=\operatorname{supp}(P_\Pi).}

Definition 3.10 (Safety set). Let \mathcal S\subseteq\mathcal T be an environmental/system safety set independent of the policy.

Definition 3.11 (Safe behavioral space).

\boxed{\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi)=\mathcal B_{\text{reach}}(\Pi)\cap\mathcal S.}

Hierarchy.

\boxed{\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi)\subseteq\mathcal B_{\text{reach}}(\Pi)\subseteq\mathcal B_{\text{poss}}.}

Remark 3.4. Version 3.0 defined \mathcal B_{\text{poss}}(\Pi)=\mathcal B(\Pi)\cap\mathcal B_{\text{env}}, which is redundant because \mathcal B(\Pi)\subseteq\mathcal B_{\text{env}}. Definition 3.8 separates the policy-independent and policy-dependent notions.

3.5 Reliability

Definition 3.12 (Initial outcome reliability).

R_{\text{init}}(\Pi)=P_\Pi\!\left[\tau\in\mathcal T_\Omega\cap\mathcal S\right].

Definition 3.13 (Verification strength and false alarm rate).

V=P(\text{detected}\mid\text{failure}),\qquad F=P(\text{detected}\mid\text{success}).

Definition 3.14 (Recovery operator). \rho maps a detected failed trajectory to a distribution over new policies.

Definition 3.15 (Recoverability).

G_R(\Pi,\rho)=P\!\left[\exists\tau'\text{ via }\rho(\tau):\Phi(\tau')\in\Omega\;\middle|\;\Phi(\tau)\notin\Omega\land\text{detected}\right].

Definition 3.16 (Effective per-episode success probability).

s=R_{\text{init}}(1-FV)+(1-R_{\text{init}})VG_R.

Axiom A2 (Recovery availability). For every detected failed trajectory, either \rho is defined or the system declares unrecoverable failure.

Axiom A3 (Episode independence). Episodes are independent. Relaxed in §8.4.

3.6 Long-Horizon Reliability

R_{\text{long}}^{(T)}(k)=\sum_{j=k}^T\binom{T}{j}s^j(1-s)^{T-j}.

3.7 Classical Embedding

Theorem 3.1 (Classical embedding). Let \Pi=\{\pi_f\} be a singleton deterministic policy computing f:X\to Y. Let \Omega(x)=\{y^\ast\}, V=1, F=0, G_R=0, |\mathcal B_{\text{reach}}(\Pi)|=1, \rho=\varnothing. Then R_{\text{init}}(\Pi)=\mathbf 1[f(x)=y^\ast], and the deterministic function-based specification model is recovered as a limiting special case.

Proof. The singleton policy induces P_\Pi(\tau_f)=1. The acceptable set is a singleton. Then R_{\text{init}}=\mathbf 1[\Phi(\tau_f)\in\Omega]=\mathbf 1[f(x)=y^\ast]. \square

Remark 3.5. This is a reduction of the correctness model, not of classical computability theory.

