Chapter 4: Behavioral Freedom

4.1 The Reference-Measure Formulation

Behavioral freedom is not an intrinsic scalar property of a policy. It is a property of a policy relative to a declared behavioral measure.

Definition 4.1 (Behavioral reference measure). Let \mu be a normalized reference measure on (\mathcal T,\Sigma_{\mathcal T}), explicitly part of the system specification.

Definition 4.2 (Behavioral freedom).

\boxed{F_\mu(\Pi)=\mu\!\left(\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi)\right).}

Remark 4.1. Under (D), \mu is counting measure and F_\mu(\Pi)=|\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi)|. Under (C), \mu is a fixed reference measure; volume is coordinate-dependent, so \mu must be declared as part of the specification.

4.2 The Four Freedoms

Structural quantities.

B_{\text{poss}}=\mu(\mathcal B_{\text{poss}}),\qquad B_{\text{reach}}(\Pi)=\mu(\mathcal B_{\text{reach}}(\Pi)).

Probabilistic quantity. Under (D):

B_\eta(\Pi)=\mu\!\left(\{\tau\in\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi):P_\Pi(\tau)\ge\eta\}\right).

Diversity quantity.

B_{\text{used}}(\Pi)=H\!\left(P_\Pi^{\text{safe}}\right)=-\sum_{\tau\in\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi)}P_\Pi^{\text{safe}}(\tau)\log P_\Pi^{\text{safe}}(\tau).

Reference-dependent quantity.

D_{\text{beh}}(\Pi;\Pi_0)=\int_{\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi)}\log\frac{p_\Pi(\tau)}{p_{\Pi_0}(\tau)}\,dP_\Pi^{\text{safe}}(\tau).

Remark 4.2. D_{\text{beh}} is not the foundational definition of behavioral freedom; it is an operational metric for comparing policies relative to a reference policy \Pi_0.

4.3 Hierarchy and Bounds

Proposition 4.1 (Hierarchy). B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi)\le B_{\text{reach}}(\Pi)\le B_{\text{poss}}.

Proposition 4.2 (Entropy bound under (D)). If P_\Pi^{\text{safe}} is supported on \mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi) with |\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}|=B_{\text{safe}}:

B_{\text{used}}(\Pi)\le\log B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi),

with equality iff P_\Pi^{\text{safe}} is uniform.

4.4 Behavioral Multiplicity vs. Semantic Multiplicity

Definition 4.3 (Semantic multiplicity).

M_{\text{sem}}(\Pi)=\left|\{[\tau]_O:\tau\in\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi),\,P_\Pi^{\text{safe}}([\tau]_O)>0\}\right|.

Definition 4.4 (Behavioral multiplicity).

M_{\text{beh}}(\Pi)=|\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi)|.

Proposition 4.3 (Multiplicity gap). M_{\text{sem}}(\Pi)\le M_{\text{beh}}(\Pi), with equality iff \Phi is injective on \mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi).

Remark 4.3. This gap is the structural reason outcome-centric systems can exploit behavioral multiplicity. Many behaviorally distinct trajectories may produce a small number of semantically distinct outcomes.

Open Question 4.1. Can behavioral freedom be increased while the number of semantically distinct outcomes remains bounded?

