Chapter 5: The Freedom–Reliability Frontier

5.1 Definition

Definition 5.1 (Feasible policy class).

\mathcal P(R_{\text{req}})=\{\Pi\in\mathcal P:R_{\text{long}}(\Pi,\rho)\ge R_{\text{req}}\}.

Definition 5.2 (Maximum achievable reliability).

R_{\max}=\sup_{\Pi\in\mathcal P}R_{\text{long}}(\Pi,\rho).

Definition 5.3 (Freedom–Reliability Frontier). For R_{\text{req}}\le R_{\max}:

\boxed{B^\ast(R_{\text{req}})=\sup_{\Pi\in\mathcal P(R_{\text{req}})}F_\mu(\Pi).}

For R_{\text{req}}>R_{\max}, the frontier is undefined.

5.2 Existence

Theorem 5.1 (Existence and attainment). Suppose:

1. \mathcal P is a compact topological space.
2. F_\mu:\mathcal P\to[0,\infty) is upper semicontinuous.
3. R_{\text{long}}:\mathcal P\to[0,1] is lower semicontinuous.
4. R_{\text{req}}\le R_{\max}.

Then for each admissible R_{\text{req}}, B^\ast(R_{\text{req}}) is attained by some \Pi^\ast\in\mathcal P(R_{\text{req}}) and is finite.

Proof. Under (3), \mathcal P(R_{\text{req}}) is closed in \mathcal P, hence compact. Under (2), an upper semicontinuous function on a non-empty compact set attains its supremum. \square

5.3 Monotonicity

Theorem 5.2 (Weak monotonicity). If R_{\text{req}}^{(1)}>R_{\text{req}}^{(2)}, then B^\ast(R_{\text{req}}^{(1)})\le B^\ast(R_{\text{req}}^{(2)}).

Proof. \mathcal P(R_{\text{req}}^{(1)})\subseteq\mathcal P(R_{\text{req}}^{(2)}). \square

5.4 Shadow Price of Reliability

Assumption 5.1 (Regularity). \mathcal P is a smooth finite-dimensional manifold; F_\mu and R_{\text{long}} are C^1; the constraint is active at \Pi^\ast(R_{\text{req}}); \nabla R_{\text{long}}(\Pi^\ast)\neq 0; and the value function is differentiable.

Theorem 5.3 (Shadow price characterization). Under Assumption 5.1:

\boxed{\frac{dB^\ast}{dR_{\text{req}}}=-\lambda^\ast(R_{\text{req}}),}

where \lambda^\ast\ge 0 is the Lagrange multiplier on the reliability constraint.

Proof. Apply the envelope theorem to \mathcal L(\Pi,\lambda)=F_\mu(\Pi)+\lambda(R_{\text{long}}(\Pi)-R_{\text{req}}). At the optimum, \nabla F_\mu+\lambda^\ast\nabla R_{\text{long}}=0. Differentiating the value function with respect to R_{\text{req}} gives dB^\ast/dR_{\text{req}}=-\lambda^\ast. \square

Corollary 5.1 (Slack regime). If the constraint is inactive, \lambda^\ast=0 and dB^\ast/dR_{\text{req}}=0.

Corollary 5.2 (Genuine tradeoff). If \lambda^\ast>0, reliability and behavioral freedom trade off strictly.

Remark 5.1. If the value function is not differentiable, use one-sided derivatives or subgradients:

\partial B^\ast(R_{\text{req}})=[-\lambda^\ast_+,\,-\lambda^\ast_-],

where \lambda^\ast_\pm are the right and left shadow prices.

5.5 Recovery Feasible-Set Expansion

Assumption 5.2 (Monotone recovery). R_{\text{long}}(\Pi,G) is non-decreasing in G for every policy \Pi.

Theorem 5.4 (Recovery feasible-set expansion). Under Assumption 5.2, if G_2\ge G_1, then

\mathcal P_{G_1}(R)\subseteq\mathcal P_{G_2}(R),

and therefore

\boxed{B^\ast_{G_2}(R)\ge B^\ast_{G_1}(R)}

for all admissible R.

Proof. Since R_{\text{long}}(\Pi,G_2)\ge R_{\text{long}}(\Pi,G_1), any policy satisfying R_{\text{long}}(\Pi,G_1)\ge R also satisfies R_{\text{long}}(\Pi,G_2)\ge R. \square

Definition 5.4 (Marginal reliability cost).

\lambda_G(R)=-\frac{\partial B^\ast_G}{\partial R}.

Conjecture 5.1 (Recovery curvature). Increasing recoverability decreases the marginal behavioral cost of reliability: \lambda_{G_2}(R)\le\lambda_{G_1}(R) for G_2>G_1.

Remark 5.2. This is stated as a conjecture. A decreasing multiplier does not by itself establish an ordering of its derivative across different values of G. The curvature claim requires a stronger parametric optimization theorem.

5.6 Phase Structure

Definition 5.5 (Phase classification).

· Stable if B^\ast(R_{\text{req}})>0 for all R_{\text{req}}<R_{\max}.
· Fragile if there exists R_{\text{req}}^c with B^\ast(R_{\text{req}})=0 for R_{\text{req}}>R_{\text{req}}^c.
· Recoverable if increasing G strictly increases B^\ast for some R_{\text{req}}.
· Unsafe if B^\ast(R_{\text{req}})=0 for all R_{\text{req}}>0.

Proposition 5.1 (Critical reliability). Let R_{\max}^{(0)}=\sup_\Pi R_{\text{init}}(\Pi). When G=0, R_{\text{req}}^c=R_{\max}^{(0)}.

