Chapter 8: Dynamic Authority as Stochastic Control

8.1 Formulation

Definition 8.1 (Authority allocation). H(s)\in[0,1] is human authority; A(s)=1-H(s).

Definition 8.2 (Authority-adjusted reliability).

R_{\text{eff}}(s,H)=HR_H(s)+(1-H)R_A(s).

8.2 Markov Decision Formulation

Definition 8.3 (Intervention cost). C(s,H) with C(s,0)=0, C(s,1)=C_H>0, strictly convex in H.

Definition 8.4 (Authority control problem).

\boxed{h^\ast=\arg\max_h\mathbb E\!\left[\sum_{t=0}^T\gamma^t\left(R_{\text{eff}}(s_t,h(s_t))-C(s_t,h(s_t))\right)\right].}

Theorem 8.1 (Existence). Under measurability of R_H,R_A,C and compactness of the state space, an optimal stationary deterministic policy h^\ast exists.

Proof. Standard MDP existence theorem for continuous action spaces with measurable reward and compact state space. \square

8.3 Interior Optimum

Theorem 8.2 (Interior optimum). Suppose C(s,\cdot) is strictly convex, \Delta R(s)=R_H(s)-R_A(s)>0, and \frac{\partial C}{\partial H}(s,0)<\Delta R(s)<\frac{\partial C}{\partial H}(s,1). Then H^\ast(s)\in(0,1) satisfies

\boxed{\frac{\partial C}{\partial H}(s,H^\ast(s))=\Delta R(s).}

Proof. First-order condition R_H-R_A-\partial C/\partial H=0. Strict convexity gives a maximum. Boundary conditions place the critical point in (0,1). \square

Remark 8.1. If R_H(s)<R_A(s), the optimum can be H^\ast(s)=0.

8.4 Authority Dynamics

Definition 8.5 (Discrete authority update).

H_{t+1}=\operatorname{Proj}_{[0,1]}\!\left[H_t+\eta(\gamma_UU_t-\gamma_CC_t)\right].

Remark 8.2. Version 3.0 projected the derivative rather than the state. The discrete formulation above is standard and correct.

