Chapter 10: Falsification Criterion and Empirical Program

10.1 Theoretical Falsification Criterion

If every major result concerning outcome-centric computing can be expressed as a straightforward reformulation of existing constrained-MDP, stochastic-control, runtime-verification, or fault-tolerant-control results without requiring the outcome-semantic quotient \mathcal M_O, then the proposed framework does not constitute a distinct theoretical abstraction.

10.2 Decisive Experiment

Construct tasks where |\mathcal T_\Omega|\gg 1 and compare them with tasks where |\mathcal T_\Omega|\approx 1. Systematically increase environmental uncertainty U. Measure the ratio

\frac{\mu(\mathcal T_\Omega\cap\mathcal S)}{\mu(\mathcal B_{\text{poss}})}

and reliability. The theory predicts that outcome-centric architecture becomes increasingly useful when (1) acceptable trajectories are numerous, (2) trajectories can differ substantially, (3) semantic equivalence remains high, (4) environmental uncertainty increases, and (5) recovery is available.

10.3 Comparative Simulation

Simulate five models:

· Model A: Procedural/deterministic specification.
· Model B: Outcome-centric without recovery.
· Model C: Outcome-centric + verification.
· Model D: Outcome-centric + verification + recovery.
· Model E: Outcome-centric + verification + recovery + dynamic authority.

Measure R, F_\mu, recovery rate, verification cost, and human-intervention cost.

10.4 Falsifiable Predictions

Prediction P1 (Theorem 5.2). B^\ast is non-increasing in R_{\text{req}}.

Prediction P2 (Theorem 6.1). s(u) peaks at u^\ast given by the three-regime formula.

Prediction P3 (Theorem 5.4). Increasing G weakly expands the frontier.

Prediction P4 (Theorem 8.2). The empirical interior optimum H^\ast(s) satisfies \partial C/\partial H=\Delta R(s).

10.5 Empirical Hypotheses

Hypothesis H1. There exists a critical autonomy A_c beyond which undetected failure rises.

Hypothesis H2. Outcome specifications outperform procedural specifications as environmental uncertainty U grows.

Hypothesis H3 (Conjecture 5.1). Recovery reduces the marginal cost of reliability.

Hypothesis H4. Coarse-grained verification at quotient boundaries achieves comparable reliability at lower cost than step-wise verification.

