Chapter 12: Conclusion

The central claim of this dissertation is that AI-native software systems introduce a computational regime in which correctness cannot be adequately characterized by prescribed input–output mappings alone. The outcome-centric model provides a formal foundation for this regime.

The deepest structural insight is that behavioral multiplicity can exceed semantic multiplicity, and that the outcome-semantic quotient space \mathcal M_O is the primitive object expressing this fact. The freedom–reliability frontier, the verification–recovery tradeoff, state-dependent recovery, and dynamic authority are the analytical apparatus built on this foundation.

The motivating question—

\boxed{\text{Under what conditions can behavioral freedom be increased without violating probabilistic guarantees on acceptable outcomes?}}

—is answered under explicit assumptions by the theorems of Chapters 5–8. The theory is falsifiable: if every result can be expressed without \mathcal M_O, then it is not a distinct abstraction. The decisive experiment—testing |\mathcal T_\Omega|\gg 1 against |\mathcal T_\Omega|\approx 1 under increasing uncertainty—will determine whether the framework earns its claim to be a theory rather than a taxonomy.

