Appendix A: Measure-Theoretic Foundations

A.1 Polish Spaces and Borel Structure

The trajectory space \mathcal T is assumed to carry a standard Borel structure. Under Assumption 3.1(D), \mathcal T is countable and the counting measure \mu is well-defined. Under Assumption 3.1(C), \mathcal T\subseteq(\mathcal X\times\mathcal A)^T\times\mathcal X is a compact subset of a finite-dimensional Euclidean space, hence Polish. The Borel \sigma-algebra \Sigma_{\mathcal T} is generated by the product topology.

Lemma A.1 (Measurability of the acceptable set). Under Axiom A2, \mathcal T_\Omega=\Phi^{-1}(\Omega)\in\Sigma_{\mathcal T}.

Proof. \Phi is measurable by Axiom A2, and \Omega\subseteq\mathcal O is Borel. Preimages of Borel sets under measurable maps are measurable. \square

A.2 Topological Support

Lemma A.2 (Existence of topological support). For any probability measure P_\Pi on the Polish space (\mathcal T,\Sigma_{\mathcal T}), the topological support

\operatorname{supp}(P_\Pi)=\{x\in\mathcal T:P_\Pi(U)>0\text{ for every open }U\ni x\}

is a closed set of full P_\Pi-measure.

Proof. Let \mathcal B be a countable base for the topology. Define

S=\mathcal T\setminus\bigcup\{B\in\mathcal B:P_\Pi(B)=0\}.

Then S is closed, P_\Pi(S)=1, and S=\{x:P_\Pi(U)>0\text{ for every open }U\ni x\}. Indeed, if x\in S and U\ni x is open, there exists B\in\mathcal B with x\in B\subseteq U; since P_\Pi(B)>0, P_\Pi(U)>0. Conversely, if x\notin S, then x\in B for some B\in\mathcal B with P_\Pi(B)=0, so x\notin\operatorname{supp}(P_\Pi). \square

A.3 Safe-Conditional Measure

Lemma A.3 (Safe-conditional measure is a probability measure). If P_\Pi(\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi))>0, then

P_\Pi^{\text{safe}}(A)=\frac{P_\Pi(A\cap\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi))}{P_\Pi(\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi))}

defines a probability measure on (\mathcal T,\Sigma_{\mathcal T}).

Proof. Non-negativity is immediate. Normalization:

P_\Pi^{\text{safe}}(\mathcal T)=\frac{P_\Pi(\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi))}{P_\Pi(\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi))}=1.

Countable additivity follows from countable additivity of P_\Pi. \square

