Appendix B: Behavioral Freedom

B.1 Hierarchy of Behavioral Spaces

Proposition B.1 (Strict hierarchy). Under Axiom A3 and Definition 3.11, \mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi)\subseteq\mathcal B_{\text{reach}}(\Pi)\subseteq\mathcal B_{\text{poss}}.

Proof. \mathcal B_{\text{reach}}(\Pi)=\operatorname{supp}(P_\Pi)\subseteq\mathcal B_{\text{env}}=\mathcal B_{\text{poss}} because policies cannot generate trajectories outside the environmentally reachable set. \mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi)=\mathcal B_{\text{reach}}(\Pi)\cap\mathcal S\subseteq\mathcal B_{\text{reach}}(\Pi) by definition. \square

B.2 Reference Measure and Volume

Definition B.1 (Behavioral reference measure). Let \mu be a \sigma-finite measure on (\mathcal T,\Sigma_{\mathcal T}), declared as part of the system specification.

Remark B.1 (Coordinate dependence). Under Assumption 3.1(C), \mu is not invariant under arbitrary diffeomorphisms of \mathcal T. The behavioral freedom F_\mu(\Pi)=\mu(\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi)) is therefore relative to the declared reference measure \mu. This is not a defect; it is a necessary feature of any volume-based notion of behavioral freedom.

Proposition B.2 (Invariance under measure-preserving transformations). If \psi:\mathcal T\to\mathcal T is a bijection with \mu\circ\psi^{-1}=\mu, then F_\mu(\psi(\Pi))=F_\mu(\Pi) where \psi(\Pi) denotes the pushforward policy.

Proof. \mu(\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\psi(\Pi)))=\mu(\psi(\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi)))=\mu(\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi)). \square

B.3 Entropy Bound

Proposition B.3 (Maximum entropy under (D)). Let P_\Pi^{\text{safe}} be supported on a finite set \mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi) with |\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}|=B_{\text{safe}}. Then

B_{\text{used}}(\Pi)=H(P_\Pi^{\text{safe}})\le\log B_{\text{safe}},

with equality iff P_\Pi^{\text{safe}} is uniform on \mathcal B_{\text{safe}}.

Proof. By Gibbs' inequality, for any probability distribution q on a finite set \mathcal S,

-\sum_{x\in\mathcal S}q(x)\log q(x)\le\log|\mathcal S|,

with equality iff q is uniform. Apply with \mathcal S=\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}. \square

B.4 Multiplicity Gap

Proposition B.4 (Multiplicity gap). M_{\text{sem}}(\Pi)\le M_{\text{beh}}(\Pi), with equality iff \Phi is injective on \mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi).

Proof. The map \tau\mapsto[\tau]_O from \mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi) to the set of outcome classes is surjective onto its image. The image has cardinality M_{\text{sem}}(\Pi), and the domain has cardinality M_{\text{beh}}(\Pi). Surjectivity implies |image|\le|domain|. Equality holds iff the map is injective, which is equivalent to \Phi being injective on \mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi). \square

