Appendix C: The Freedom–Reliability Frontier

C.1 Attainment Under Compactness

Theorem C.1 (Weierstrass attainment). Let \mathcal P be a compact topological space, F_\mu:\mathcal P\to\mathbb R upper semicontinuous, and R_{\text{long}}:\mathcal P\to\mathbb R lower semicontinuous. For any R_{\text{req}}\le R_{\max}, the set

\mathcal P(R_{\text{req}})=\{\Pi\in\mathcal P:R_{\text{long}}(\Pi)\ge R_{\text{req}}\}

is non-empty and compact, and F_\mu attains its supremum on \mathcal P(R_{\text{req}}).

Proof. Non-emptiness follows from R_{\text{req}}\le R_{\max}=\sup_{\Pi}R_{\text{long}}(\Pi). Compactness: \mathcal P(R_{\text{req}})=R_{\text{long}}^{-1}([R_{\text{req}},\infty)). Since R_{\text{long}} is lower semicontinuous, [R_{\text{req}},\infty) is closed in \mathbb R, so its preimage is closed in \mathcal P, hence compact. Upper semicontinuous functions on non-empty compact sets attain their suprema. \square

C.2 Shadow Price via Envelope Theorem

Theorem C.2 (Envelope theorem for the frontier). Under Assumption 5.1, the value function B^\ast(R_{\text{req}}) is differentiable and

\frac{dB^\ast}{dR_{\text{req}}}=-\lambda^\ast(R_{\text{req}}).

Proof. Consider the Lagrangian

\mathcal L(\Pi,\lambda)=F_\mu(\Pi)+\lambda(R_{\text{long}}(\Pi)-R_{\text{req}}).

At the optimum \Pi^\ast(R_{\text{req}}), the first-order condition gives \nabla F_\mu(\Pi^\ast)+\lambda^\ast\nabla R_{\text{long}}(\Pi^\ast)=0. The value function is B^\ast(R_{\text{req}})=\mathcal L(\Pi^\ast(R_{\text{req}}),\lambda^\ast(R_{\text{req}})). Differentiating with respect to R_{\text{req}} and using the envelope theorem:

\frac{dB^\ast}{dR_{\text{req}}}=\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial R_{\text{req}}}(\Pi^\ast,\lambda^\ast)=-\lambda^\ast,

where the terms involving \partial\Pi^\ast/\partial R_{\text{req}} and \partial\lambda^\ast/\partial R_{\text{req}} vanish by the first-order conditions. \square

C.3 Nonsmooth Extension

Definition C.1 (Clarke subdifferential). For a locally Lipschitz function f:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R, the Clarke subdifferential at x is

\partial f(x)=\operatorname{co}\left\{\lim_{k\to\infty}\nabla f(x_k):x_k\to x,\,x_k\in\Omega_f\right\},

where \Omega_f is the set of points where f is differentiable.

Theorem C.3 (Nonsmooth envelope theorem). If B^\ast is locally Lipschitz and directionally differentiable, then for any direction d\in\mathbb R,

B^{\ast\prime}(R_{\text{req}};d)=\min_{\lambda\in\Lambda^\ast(R_{\text{req}})}(-\lambda d),

where \Lambda^\ast(R_{\text{req}}) is the set of optimal Lagrange multipliers.

Proof. See Clarke (1990, Theorem 2.8.6) and Morand, Reffett, and Tarafdar (2012). The directional derivative of the value function equals the minimum over optimal multipliers of the directional derivative of the Lagrangian. \square

C.4 Recovery Feasible-Set Expansion

Theorem C.4 (Recovery feasible-set expansion). Under Assumption 5.2, if G_2\ge G_1, then \mathcal P_{G_1}(R)\subseteq\mathcal P_{G_2}(R) and B^\ast_{G_2}(R)\ge B^\ast_{G_1}(R).

Proof. Let \Pi\in\mathcal P_{G_1}(R). Then R_{\text{long}}(\Pi,G_1)\ge R. Since R_{\text{long}}(\Pi,G) is non-decreasing in G, R_{\text{long}}(\Pi,G_2)\ge R_{\text{long}}(\Pi,G_1)\ge R. Thus \Pi\in\mathcal P_{G_2}(R). The frontier inequality follows because the supremum over a larger set is at least as large. \square

