Appendix D: Verification–Recovery Tradeoff

D.1 Strict Concavity

Lemma D.1. Let \alpha,\beta>0, G\in(0,1), B>0, and

s(u)=1-(1-G)e^{-\alpha u}-Ge^{-\beta B}e^{(\beta-\alpha)u},\qquad u\in[0,B].

Then s''(u)<0 for all u\in[0,B].

Proof. Compute:

s'(u)=(1-G)\alpha e^{-\alpha u}+G(\alpha-\beta)e^{-\alpha u-\beta(B-u)}.

s''(u)=-(1-G)\alpha^2e^{-\alpha u}-G(\beta-\alpha)^2e^{-\beta B}e^{(\beta-\alpha)u}.

Since (1-G)>0, \alpha^2>0, e^{-\alpha u}>0, the first term is negative. The second term is non-positive because G(\beta-\alpha)^2\ge 0 and e^{-\beta B}e^{(\beta-\alpha)u}>0. The sum is strictly negative because the first term is strictly negative. \square

D.2 Three-Regime Classification

Theorem D.2 (Three-regime allocation). The unique maximizer u^\ast of s on [0,B] satisfies exactly one of:

(I) \beta\le\alpha or G\le\alpha/\beta: u^\ast=B.

(II) \beta>\alpha and \frac{\alpha}{\beta}<G<\frac{\alpha e^{\beta B}}{\beta-\alpha+\alpha e^{\beta B}}: u^\ast=B-\frac1\beta\ln\left(\frac{G(\beta-\alpha)}{(1-G)\alpha}\right)\in(0,B).

(III) \beta>\alpha and G\ge\frac{\alpha e^{\beta B}}{\beta-\alpha+\alpha e^{\beta B}}: u^\ast=0.

Proof. By Lemma D.1, s is strictly concave, so the unconstrained critical point is the unique global maximizer over \mathbb R. The critical point satisfies s'(u^\ast)=0:

(1-G)\alpha e^{-\alpha u^\ast}=G(\beta-\alpha)e^{-\alpha u^\ast-\beta(B-u^\ast)}.

Divide by e^{-\alpha u^\ast}:

(1-G)\alpha=G(\beta-\alpha)e^{-\beta(B-u^\ast)}.

If \beta=\alpha, then s'(u)=(1-G)\alpha e^{-\alpha u}>0 for all u, so u^\ast=B. If \beta<\alpha, then G(\alpha-\beta)e^{-\alpha u-\beta(B-u)}<0, and s'(u)=(1-G)\alpha e^{-\alpha u}-G(\beta-\alpha)e^{-\alpha u-\beta(B-u)}>0, so u^\ast=B. If \beta>\alpha, solve:

e^{-\beta(B-u^\ast)}=\frac{(1-G)\alpha}{G(\beta-\alpha)}.

u^\ast=B-\frac1\beta\ln\left(\frac{G(\beta-\alpha)}{(1-G)\alpha}\right).

The condition u^\ast<B is equivalent to \frac{G(\beta-\alpha)}{(1-G)\alpha}>1, i.e., G>\alpha/\beta. The condition u^\ast>0 is equivalent to \frac{G(\beta-\alpha)}{(1-G)\alpha}<e^{\beta B}, i.e.,

G<\frac{\alpha e^{\beta B}}{\beta-\alpha+\alpha e^{\beta B}}.

If G\le\alpha/\beta, then u^\ast\ge B, so the constrained maximizer is B. If G\ge\frac{\alpha e^{\beta B}}{\beta-\alpha+\alpha e^{\beta B}}, then u^\ast\le 0, so the constrained maximizer is 0. \square

D.3 Comparative Statics

Proposition D.3. Under Regime II:

\frac{\partial u^\ast}{\partial B}=1,\qquad \frac{\partial u^\ast}{\partial\alpha}>0,\qquad \frac{\partial u^\ast}{\partial G}<0.

Proof. The expression

u^\ast=B-\frac1\beta\ln\left(\frac{G(\beta-\alpha)}{(1-G)\alpha}\right)

has B appearing only in the first term. Therefore \partial u^\ast/\partial B=1. For \alpha:

\frac{\partial u^\ast}{\partial\alpha}=-\frac1\beta\cdot\frac{(1-G)\alpha}{G(\beta-\alpha)}\cdot\frac{\partial}{\partial\alpha}\left(\frac{G(\beta-\alpha)}{(1-G)\alpha}\right).

Compute:

\frac{\partial}{\partial\alpha}\left(\frac{\beta-\alpha}{\alpha}\right)=\frac{\partial}{\partial\alpha}\left(\frac{\beta}{\alpha}-1\right)=-\frac{\beta}{\alpha^2}<0.

Thus \partial u^\ast/\partial\alpha>0. For G:

\frac{\partial u^\ast}{\partial G}=-\frac1\beta\cdot\frac{(1-G)\alpha}{G(\beta-\alpha)}\cdot\frac{\partial}{\partial G}\left(\frac{G}{1-G}\right).

\frac{\partial}{\partial G}\left(\frac{G}{1-G}\right)=\frac{1}{(1-G)^2}>0.

Thus \partial u^\ast/\partial G<0. \square

