Appendix E: State-Dependent Recovery

E.1 Recovery-Modified Kernel

Definition E.1 (Recovery kernel). Let \rho be a probability measure on the policy space \mathcal P. The recovery-modified transition kernel is

P_\rho(s_{t+1}\mid s_t,a_t,\text{failure})=\int_{\mathcal P}P_\pi(s_{t+1}\mid s_t,a_t)\,d\rho(\pi).

Lemma E.2 (Well-definedness). If P_\pi(s_{t+1}\mid s_t,a_t) is measurable in \pi for each (s_t,a_t,s_{t+1}), then P_\rho is a well-defined stochastic kernel.

Proof. For fixed (s_t,a_t), the map \pi\mapsto P_\pi(s_{t+1}\mid s_t,a_t) is measurable. Integrating against the probability measure \rho yields a measurable function of s_{t+1}. Non-negativity and normalization follow from those of P_\pi. \square

E.2 Basin of Attraction

Definition E.2 (Basin of attraction). Let G\subseteq\mathcal X be a goal set. The basin of attraction under kernel P is

\mathcal A(G)=\{s\in\mathcal X:P[\exists t\ge 0:s_t\in G\mid s_0=s]>0\}.

Lemma E.3 (Monotonicity of basin). If G_1\subseteq G_2, then \mathcal A(G_1)\subseteq\mathcal A(G_2).

Proof. If s\in\mathcal A(G_1), there exists a trajectory from s reaching G_1. Since G_1\subseteq G_2, the same trajectory reaches G_2. \square

E.3 Conjecture Statement

Conjecture E.4 (State-dependent recovery dominance). Let P be the nominal kernel and P_\rho the recovery-modified kernel. If P_\rho is basin-dominant over P in the sense that \pi_\rho(\mathcal A(G))\ge\pi(\mathcal A(G)), then there exists a policy \Pi_2 with R_{\text{init}}(\Pi_2)<R_{\text{init}}(\Pi_1) but R_{\text{long}}^{\text{SD}}(\Pi_2,\rho)>R_{\text{long}}^{\text{SD}}(\Pi_1,\rho).

Remark E.1. This conjecture requires ergodic-theoretic arguments. A proof would establish that the recovery-modified invariant distribution places sufficient mass in the basin of attraction to compensate for lower initial reliability.

