Appendix F: Dynamic Authority

F.1 Existence of Optimal Stationary Policy

Theorem F.1 (MDP existence). Consider the MDP with state space \mathcal X, action space [0,1], reward

r(s,H)=R_{\text{eff}}(s,H)-C(s,H),

and discount factor \gamma\in[0,1). If \mathcal X is compact, r is upper semicontinuous in H for each s, and measurable in s for each H, then there exists an optimal stationary deterministic policy h^\ast:\mathcal X\to[0,1].

Proof. This is a standard MDP existence theorem for continuous action spaces. The compactness of \mathcal X and upper semicontinuity of r ensure the existence of an optimal selector by the measurable selection theorem. See Puterman (1994, Theorem 6.2.10). \square

F.2 Interior Optimum

Theorem F.2 (Interior optimum). Suppose C(s,\cdot) is strictly convex, \Delta R(s)=R_H(s)-R_A(s)>0, and

\frac{\partial C}{\partial H}(s,0)<\Delta R(s)<\frac{\partial C}{\partial H}(s,1).

Then there exists a unique H^\ast(s)\in(0,1) satisfying

\frac{\partial C}{\partial H}(s,H^\ast(s))=\Delta R(s).

Proof. Define \phi(H)=R_{\text{eff}}(s,H)-C(s,H). Then \phi'(H)=R_H(s)-R_A(s)-\frac{\partial C}{\partial H}(s,H). By strict convexity of C, \frac{\partial C}{\partial H} is strictly increasing in H. Thus \phi' is strictly decreasing. The boundary conditions give \phi'(0)>0 and \phi'(1)<0. By the intermediate value theorem, there exists a unique H^\ast\in(0,1) with \phi'(H^\ast)=0. Since \phi''<0, this is a maximum. \square

F.3 Authority Dynamics

Definition F.1 (Discrete authority update).

H_{t+1}=\operatorname{Proj}_{[0,1]}\left[H_t+\eta(\gamma_UU_t-\gamma_CC_t)\right].

Lemma F.3 (Boundedness). For any H_0\in[0,1] and any sequences (U_t), (C_t), the update F.1 satisfies H_t\in[0,1] for all t\ge 0.

Proof. The projection \operatorname{Proj}_{[0,1]}(x)=\max(0,\min(1,x)) maps \mathbb R to [0,1]. By induction, H_t\in[0,1] implies H_{t+1}\in[0,1]. \square

