Appendix G: Classical Embedding

Theorem G.1 (Correctness-model reduction). Let \Pi=\{\pi_f\} be a singleton deterministic policy computing f:X\to Y. Let

\Omega(x)=\{y^\ast\},\quad V=1,\quad F=0,\quad G_R=0,\quad|\mathcal B_{\text{reach}}(\Pi)|=1,\quad\rho=\varnothing.

Then

R_{\text{init}}(\Pi)=\mathbf 1[f(x)=y^\ast].

Proof. The singleton policy induces P_\Pi(\tau_f)=1 where \tau_f is the unique trajectory generated by \pi_f. The acceptable set is \Omega=\{y^\ast\}. Since V=1, F=0, G_R=0,

s=R_{\text{init}}(1-0)+(1-R_{\text{init}})\cdot 1\cdot 0=R_{\text{init}}.

And

R_{\text{init}}=P_\Pi[\Phi(\tau)\in\Omega]=P_\Pi[\Phi(\tau_f)=y^\ast]=\mathbf 1[\Phi(\tau_f)=y^\ast]=\mathbf 1[f(x)=y^\ast].

\square

Remark G.1. This theorem establishes that the deterministic function-based specification model is recovered as a limiting special case of the outcome-centric correctness model. It does not establish a reduction of computability theory.

