Appendix H: Multi-Agent Stability Constraint

H.1 Switched Systems

Definition H.1 (Switched system). A switched system is a family of dynamical subsystems

\dot x(t)=f_{\sigma(t)}(x(t)),\qquad \sigma:[0,\infty)\to\mathcal P,

where \mathcal P is a finite index set.

Definition H.2 (Average dwell time). A switching signal \sigma has average dwell time \tau_a if there exists N_0\ge 0 such that for all t\ge s\ge 0,

N_\sigma(t,s)\le N_0+\frac{t-s}{\tau_a},

where N_\sigma(t,s) is the number of switches on [s,t).

H.2 Stability Theorem

Theorem H.3 (Average dwell-time stability). Suppose each mode p\in\mathcal P is exponentially stable with rate \lambda_0, i.e., there exist positive definite V_p and constants c_1,c_2,\lambda_0>0 such that

c_1\|x\|^2\le V_p(x)\le c_2\|x\|^2,\qquad \dot V_p(x)\le-\lambda_0 V_p(x).

Suppose further that there exists \mu\ge 1 such that

V_p(x)\le\mu V_q(x)\qquad\text{for all }p,q\in\mathcal P,\ x\in\mathbb R^n.

Then the switched system is globally uniformly asymptotically stable under any switching signal with average dwell time

\tau_a>\frac{\ln\mu}{\lambda_0}.

Proof. See Liberzon (2003, Theorem 3.2) and the weighted average persistent dwell-time framework of the switched-systems literature. The Lyapunov function decays at rate \lambda_0 during each mode and experiences a jump of at most \mu at each switch. The average dwell-time condition ensures the decay dominates the jumps. \square

Remark H.1. This result provides a principled architectural constraint for multi-agent handoff: if the switching frequency exceeds 1/\tau_a, stability is not guaranteed, and the orchestrator should deny the handoff.

