Appendix I: Falsification Criterion

I.1 Non-Reducibility

Definition I.1 (Reducibility). A result of outcome-centric computing is reducible if it can be stated and proved entirely within the framework of constrained MDPs, stochastic control, runtime verification, or fault-tolerant control, without invoking the outcome-semantic quotient \mathcal M_O=\mathcal T/\!\sim_O.

Falsification Criterion I.1. If every major result of outcome-centric computing is reducible in the sense of Definition I.1, then the proposed framework does not constitute a distinct theoretical abstraction.

Proposition I.2 (Non-reducibility of the multiplicity gap). The multiplicity gap M_{\text{sem}}(\Pi)\le M_{\text{beh}}(\Pi) (Proposition B.4) is not reducible to constrained-MDP language.

Proof. The multiplicity gap is a statement about the quotient map \Phi:\mathcal T\to\mathcal O and the equivalence relation \equiv_O on \mathcal O. Constrained MDPs operate on state-action spaces with scalar rewards; they do not possess a trajectory-level quotient by outcome equivalence. The gap is therefore a structural property of the outcome-semantic quotient space \mathcal M_O. \square

Proposition I.3 (Non-reducibility of the freedom–reliability frontier). The frontier B^\ast(R_{\text{req}}) with freedom functional F_\mu(\Pi)=\mu(\mathcal B_{\text{safe}}(\Pi)) is not reducible to a standard constrained-MDP Pareto frontier.

Proof. The constrained-MDP Pareto frontier is defined over expected cumulative rewards and costs. The freedom functional F_\mu is a measure-theoretic volume on the safe behavioral space, not an expected reward. While a constrained MDP can represent F_\mu as an additional cost signal, the resulting frontier depends on the trajectory-level measure \mu, which is not a standard MDP primitive. The quotient structure \mathcal M_O is required to define the relationship between behavioral multiplicity and semantic multiplicity. \square

