Appendix J: Proof of Stochastic Dominance for Binomial Distributions

Theorem J.1 (Binomial stochastic dominance). Let X\sim\text{Binomial}(T,s_1) and Y\sim\text{Binomial}(T,s_2) with s_2>s_1. Then X\le_{\text{st}}Y, i.e., P(X\ge k)\le P(Y\ge k) for all k\in\{0,1,\dots,T\}.

Proof. By the likelihood ratio ordering, the binomial family is stochastically increasing in the success parameter. Explicitly, for s_2>s_1 and j>i,

\frac{P(Y=j)P(X=i)}{P(Y=i)P(X=j)}=\frac{\binom Tj s_2^j(1-s_2)^{T-j}\binom Ti s_1^i(1-s_1)^{T-i}}{\binom Ti s_2^i(1-s_2)^{T-i}\binom Tj s_1^j(1-s_1)^{T-j}}
=\left(\frac{s_2(1-s_1)}{s_1(1-s_2)}\right)^{j-i}>1.

The monotone likelihood ratio property implies first-order stochastic dominance (Klenke and Mattner, 2010). \square

Corollary J.2. If s_2>s_1, then E[X]=Ts_2>Ts_1=E[Y].

Proof. E[X]=Ts_1 and E[Y]=Ts_2. \square

